=== entry ca155dd357b3
kind: translation
rules: isolation, verify-surface, team-kit, cursor-only, model-effort, resume, stop, same-model-review
intent: Workers get their own worktree on this machine in place of a cloud VM, so the list of reasons to stay local becomes a note that runtime checks, transcripts, simulators, and local auth work from any worker; control-ui and control-cli follow rule verify-surface. Effort is the role line's ceiling. Forking, which T3 lacks, becomes a new worker briefed with the old worker's worktree path (no rule covers forking). A follow-up message is t3_thread_send and a status check is task_status. Policy: the verifier runs on the owner's model and effort (rule same-model-review). Preserve one owner per PR, fresh workers with full kickoffs, the three-level cap and rolling window, and starting with one unit.
--- upstream
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Run workers on a new cloud VM (`environment: "cloud"`) unless the work needs this machine: a `control-ui` or `control-cli` runtime check (from `cursor-team-kit`), local transcripts, a simulator, or auth that exists only here. Pick each subagent's reasoning level and fast or standard speed for the job, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Fork when new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process. Message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on a different model family. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
--- patched
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Run each worker with `isolation: "worktree"`, a checkout of your current local commit, and name the branch it checks out first. Uncommitted edits are not copied, so commit first or name a pushed branch. Every child runs on this machine, so a runtime check (the project's own verification skill, T3's `preview_*` tools, or the shell for a CLI), transcripts, a simulator, and local auth work from any worker. Set each subagent's effort for the job, lowering its line's effort for routine work and never raising it, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Start a new worker from a worker's built state when new work needs it,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process: every child runs on this machine, so the brief gives that worktree's absolute path. Message a worker with `t3_thread_send` to its `childThreadId` only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent. Read `task_status` instead, which does not wake it.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on the same model and effort as the owner, unless the user named another. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.

=== entry 850c1a1d2385
kind: translation
rules: stop, cursor-only
intent: The read-only liveness probe uses `task_status`, which reads state without waking the agent. The Cursor-restart bullet becomes a T3 restart: no rule covers it, so it keeps upstream's recovery steps and drops the local-versus-cloud split, since every child runs on this machine and its worktree and pushed branches outlive the session. Preserve never resuming to check on an agent, the retry-by-mode table, and reattaching by PR and branch rather than agent id.
--- upstream
- Never resume an agent to check on it. A resume restarts an idle agent. Probe read-only: the ledger, `units.tsv`, `gh`, pushed branches, the cloud agent's status in the Cursor dashboard. Transcript mtime is not liveness.
- A silent death gets a synthetic postmortem row in the inbox (unit, failure mode, last evidence, options). Replan on evidence as it arrives. Never wait for full quiescence.
- Retry by mode: cap-hit or oom, respawn with smaller scope. Network-drop, retry as-is. Tool-error, retry on a different model. Unknown, retry once. Two retries, then abandon the unit and replan around it.
- A zombie that returns hours late reconciles against the current frontier and ledger before anything is accepted. Salvage unique findings through a fresh unit, never a blind merge.
- When continued spawning would produce garbage tree-wide (bad upstream output, broken acceptance, dead infra), write a stop line at the top of the standing orders, let in-flight work finish, fix the cause, clear it.
- Bound your own infra retries the same way you bound a child's. After a few consecutive tool aborts, stop retrying. Write a terminal handoff to durable state (what is done, where it lives, the exact command to resume) and end the run.
- After a Cursor restart: local agents are dead, cloud work is not. Re-read the standing orders and `units.tsv`, recompute the frontier, reattach cloud work by PR and branch rather than agent id, respawn one sub-coordinator per track from its stored brief plus current state, drain, resume. The dead session's store lock clears itself on the next write. `orch` replaces a lock whose holder pid is gone.
--- patched
- Never resume an agent to check on it. A resume restarts an idle agent. Probe read-only: the ledger, `units.tsv`, `gh`, pushed branches, the agent's `task_status`. Transcript mtime is not liveness.
- A silent death gets a synthetic postmortem row in the inbox (unit, failure mode, last evidence, options). Replan on evidence as it arrives. Never wait for full quiescence.
- Retry by mode: cap-hit or oom, respawn with smaller scope. Network-drop, retry as-is. Tool-error, retry on a different model. Unknown, retry once. Two retries, then abandon the unit and replan around it.
- A zombie that returns hours late reconciles against the current frontier and ledger before anything is accepted. Salvage unique findings through a fresh unit, never a blind merge.
- When continued spawning would produce garbage tree-wide (bad upstream output, broken acceptance, dead infra), write a stop line at the top of the standing orders, let in-flight work finish, fix the cause, clear it.
- Bound your own infra retries the same way you bound a child's. After a few consecutive tool aborts, stop retrying. Write a terminal handoff to durable state (what is done, where it lives, the exact command to resume) and end the run.
- After a T3 restart: running agents may be dead, their worktrees, commits, and pushed branches are not. Check each child's `task_status`. Re-read the standing orders and `units.tsv`, recompute the frontier, reattach work by PR and branch rather than agent id, respawn one sub-coordinator per track from its stored brief plus current state, drain, resume. The dead session's store lock clears itself on the next write. `orch` replaces a lock whose holder pid is gone.

=== entry da6d7e9ea334
kind: translation
rules: isolation, cursor-only
intent: Every child runs in a worktree on this machine, so the lost-work example is a worktree removed or the machine dying, not a cloud VM. Keep the rule that output is externalized the moment it lands, and the three examples of externalizing.
--- upstream
A unit is not done until its output is externalized the moment it lands, never batched to the end of the run. A worker pushes its branch, a verifier writes its ledger row, receipts land in the store. Work that exists only on one VM when that VM dies was never done.
--- patched
A unit is not done until its output is externalized the moment it lands, never batched to the end of the run. A worker pushes its branch, a verifier writes its ledger row, receipts land in the store. Work that exists only in one worktree when that worktree is removed or the machine dies was never done.

=== entry a3856a70a3d4
kind: policy
rules: same-model-review
intent: Policy: a dedicated verifier runs on its worker's model, not a different family, unless the user names another (rule same-model-review).
--- upstream
Scale verification to the unit. When VERIFY is a single cheap command, the worker runs it and reports the output, and the coordinator spot-checks receipts. A dedicated verifier agent (on a different model family than the worker) is for units whose verification is expensive, judgment-laden, or high-blast-radius. A verifier agent whose entire product would be rerunning one command is ceremony, not verification.
--- patched
Scale verification to the unit. When VERIFY is a single cheap command, the worker runs it and reports the output, and the coordinator spot-checks receipts. A dedicated verifier agent (on the same model as the worker, unless the user named another) is for units whose verification is expensive, judgment-laden, or high-blast-radius. A verifier agent whose entire product would be rerunning one command is ceremony, not verification.

=== entry 651cc2734134
kind: translation
rules: delegate, background, store, pr-wait, loop
intent: Workers spawn as async delegate_task children whose task IDs the coordinator keeps; the Cursor Project tracker and store become the shared pstack state folder with the tracker in its notes.md; PR and CI event subscriptions become watch_pull_request with a schedule_task heartbeat for merges. Keep no scripts, no polling loops, and no relaying. Also carries the reload-the-orchestrator-after policy: the coordinator rewrites the project's orchestrator-state.md at each drain, which the compact hook in references/compact-reload.md prints back after compaction. Upstream's store file list, where that line lived, is gone.
--- upstream
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `Task`. Keep the program's state in the Project tracker (`notes.md`) and the Project store. Watch each PR with PR and CI event subscriptions when your harness has them. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.
--- patched
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `delegate_task`, `mode: "async"`, each with its whole kickoff in `task` and its own `clientRequestId`, and keep each returned `taskId` and `childThreadId`. Keep the program's state in the store, `~/.local/state/pstack/<repo>/`, where `<repo>` is `basename "$(dirname "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)")"`, with the tracker in its `notes.md`. Create it if missing. Watch each PR with `watch_pull_request`, then end the turn. It wakes you on a failed check, required checks passing, another person's comment or review, or a new conflict. A merge ends the watch without a wake, so pair it with a `schedule_task` heartbeat every 20 to 30 minutes while you wait for merges. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.

Also rewrite `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` at each drain, under 6,000 characters. It is the one file that survives compaction, so it lives in the project and not the store. It holds the goal and the user's standing instructions, the live agents with their `taskId`s, the open PRs, the open gates, and the next steps. The compact hook in `references/compact-reload.md` prints it back; install that hook in the project's `.claude/settings.json`, or the file is never read.
